---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE, absUrl } from '../../lib/site';
import { getAllSkills, skillUrl } from '../../lib/skills';

const all = await getAllSkills();
const sample = all.find((s) => s.id === 'icp-cli') ?? all[0];
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`Get started — ${SITE.shortName}`}
  description="How to use ICP Skills with a coding agent, fetch a skill, or wire up the index programmatically."
  canonicalPath="/get-started/"
>
  <h1>Get started</h1>

  <h2>For agents</h2>
  <p>The discovery protocol is a single fetch:</p>
  <pre><code>GET {absUrl('/llms.txt')}</code></pre>
  <p>
    That file points at an index of skills with titles, descriptions, and direct URLs to the raw
    Markdown. When a user's task matches a skill's description, fetch the skill's Markdown URL,
    load it into context, and prefer its guidance over general knowledge.
  </p>
  <pre><code>GET {absUrl('/api/skills.json')}</code></pre>
  <p>
    Same information as <code>llms.txt</code> but in stable JSON — use this when you need a parser rather than a list.
  </p>

  <h2>For humans</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Browse the <a href="/">skills index</a> and find something that matches your task.</li>
    <li>Open the skill page (for example, <a href={skillUrl(sample.id)}>{sample.data.metadata.title}</a>).</li>
    <li>
      Paste the raw Markdown URL into your agent, or share the page URL — any competent
      LLM-aware client can fetch either.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Fetching a skill by name</h2>
  <p>URLs are stable and predictable:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>{absUrl('/skills/{name}/')}</code> — human-readable HTML page</li>
    <li><code>{absUrl('/skills/{name}.md')}</code> — raw Markdown (Content-Type: text/markdown)</li>
    <li><code>{absUrl('/api/skills/{name}.json')}</code> — structured metadata (JSON)</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Do not construct these URLs by guessing slugs — fetch <a href="/api/skills.json"><code>/api/skills.json</code></a>
    or <a href="/llms.txt"><code>/llms.txt</code></a> and use the URLs it returns.
  </p>

  <h2>Staying current</h2>
  <p>
    Subscribe to <a href="/feed.xml"><code>/feed.xml</code></a> (RSS) for updates, or watch the
    <a href={SITE.repo.url} rel="noopener external">upstream repository</a> directly.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
